End a text field's IME composition when focus leaves it.

What: at the start of `Window::draw`, the previous frame's input handler is taken a few lines earlier than before;
if focus has left its field (`focus_id` differs from `Window::focus`: blur, or another element focused), the field's
composition is ended (`PlatformInputHandler::end_composition_during_update`: `unmark_text` when
`marked_text_range` is `Some`) before the draw drops the handler. Its marked text stays as plain text. When it unmarked something, the draw then
calls the new `PlatformWindow::end_composition` (no-op by default), so the native IME ends its side too: macOS
`discardMarkedText` on the view's input context; web blur-and-refocus of the hidden IME mirror while the browser is
composing (a blur commits the composition), then a mirror sync. iOS and Android keep the no-op: their sessions
already restart when the field changes or blurs (iOS `invalidate_session` and `textWillChange`/`textDidChange`,
Android `setClient`). Adds an input test with one row per focus move (another element, blur, a tap on another
element, nothing marked, the same element), checking the field's marked text, its `unmark_text` calls and the
platform's `end_composition` calls.

Why: a field left mid-composition kept its marked text: it stayed underlined, and when focus came back the platform
handed that range to a new IME session, whose next keystroke replaced it (Android, Gboard Korean: ㅏ then ㅁ gave ㅁ).
Platforms can't end it themselves: they learn of the focus change only from the draw, after the left field's handler
is gone, and a tap's focus change is drawn inside the touch dispatch (`draw_before_touch_release`), so no platform
call runs between the two. Core already ends a composition when focus moves to a native view
(`end_composition_before_focusing_native_view`, deferred until the update ends); a deferred call would reach the new
field's handler after a tap's inline draw, so this runs in the draw, where no entity is being updated. Flutter's
`EditableText` does the same on blur (`clearComposing`). Unmarking alone left macOS and web composing: macOS's input
method still held the composition and its next keystroke inserted it again at the caret ("かな" then x gave
"かなかなx"), and gpui_web's mirror sync stays off while the browser composes, so the next `compositionupdate` went
to the newly focused field. The core change is not platform-specific, so no cfg; each platform's part sits in its
own crate.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

